## Use and boundaries

`/worth-it` evaluates initial value; `/worth-it continue` reassesses remaining value.
This is a weekly review lens, distinct from `/weekly-planner` and its shared daily engine.
It does not run that planner, install anything, schedule reviews or authorize a proof or post.
Helpers are optional: upstream's `ponytail-refined` folder declares callable `ponytail`;
Forge uses `ponytail` and `sanity-check`. Resolve by installed frontmatter name, disclose
missing helpers, and apply the source's standalone fallback instead of importing duplicates.

The target project's existing leaderboard/results issue is discovered at use time.
Forge issue 1118 is one contextual CI-experiment example, never a default destination:
https://github.com/HiQS-Labs/XYZ-forge/issues/1118
Preserve the source's exact-action authorization, decline and pending-capture rules.

## Update without drift

1. Inspect the upstream PR's live state and choose an explicit full source revision. If the PR merged, inspect the merged tree; do not assume it equals the old head. Review the skill diff, upstream README/license and relevant history before copying.
2. Fetch `03-improve/worth-it/SKILL.md` and `LICENSE` at that exact revision (for example with `gh api repos/Claude-AI-Tools-Ventura-County/giant-brains-claude-skills/contents/<path>?ref=<full-sha>`, decoding the returned content). Compare with this bundle. Fetch to scratch first and reject empty/failed downloads.
3. Prefer upstream changes for shared behavior; update this one bundle in a normal Forge task/PR, never a deployed copy. Preserve initial and continuation behavior together; record any deliberate local differences here rather than adding a second implementation.
4. Refresh the revision, live-state/date note, attribution if changed, license and both SHA-256 values here. Re-run applicable existing gates and honestly labeled manual walkthroughs, including results approval/decline/unknown lookup and pending closure. Obtain normal bounded review. Installation and publishing to downstream consumers are separate operator requests.

No auto-sync tooling or new vendoring framework is introduced.
